-- 0035_v1_corpus_drills.sql
--
-- Backfills the v1 corpus drills (40 rows) that never had a portable
-- migration. v1 drills only ever lived in supabase/seed.sql (PR #432,
-- dev-only via `supabase db reset`). Migration 0034 ported the v2 fill
-- (85 rows) on the assumption the 40 v1 rows were already there — true on
-- dev (seed runs at reset), false on prod. The result: prod's baseline
-- practice plans rendered the literal "Drill" for any block whose corpus
-- pick was a v1 entry.
--
-- Idempotent: `on conflict (name) do nothing` skips any row whose `name`
-- already exists. Safe to re-run; a no-op on dev (where all 40 are present).
-- Relies on the drills_name_unique constraint added in 0034.
--
-- Pre-existing v0 pollution on prod (24 lowercase / placeholder drill names
-- like 'Gate drill', 'Lag putting clock', 'Sand-shot 10s') is NOT touched
-- by this migration to avoid orphaning practice_plans JSONB references
-- that may cite those uuids. Cleanup is deferred to a separate migration
-- once we've confirmed no live plans depend on them.
--
-- Row payloads captured from dev (project txquvfeyvkaetqlamqlz) on
-- 2026-05-29 and mirror the public corpus content rendered in /practice.

insert into public.drills (
  name, description, duration_min, category, facility, skill_levels,
  instructions, goals, targets, drill_type, source, source_url,
  contributor, verified
) values
($drill$2-Ball Bounce Pitch$drill$, $drill$Set two balls in your stance and pitch through both to train the flat, skimming bounce-contact that replaces steep, choppy wedge strikes.$drill$, 15, 'around_green', '{short_game,range}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Brech Spradley (GOLF Top 100 Teacher) designed this to solve the most common pitching fault: approaching too steeply, which drives the leading edge into the turf for chunked, hosel-heavy, or armsy pitches. The two-ball setup — one ball slightly forward of center, one near the front foot — forces a shallow, sweeping arc. To contact both with one swing the club must skim along the turf rather than stab down. Spradley emphasizes keeping "a longer flat spot" and feeling "the bounce skimming the grass" rather than digging — like landing a plane along the ground, not arriving steeply. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who strike longer clubs well but get steep or choppy with short wedges inside 40 yards; players who struggle with soft-landing finesse pitches where bounce — not leading-edge contact — is the mechanism. **Setup.** On a mat, turf, or grassy area: one ball just forward of center, a second near the front foot, ~6–8 inches apart along the target line. Normal pitching stance with a 56°–60° wedge, slight forward weight, handle just ahead of the lead ball. **How.** Make a normal pitch swing, contacting both balls in one continuous swing; the arc that catches both feels shallow and sweeping. Clip the back ball then brush the front = correct. Only the front = flipping. Top or miss both = lifting. 10 two-ball sets, then remove the front ball and hit 10 single-ball pitches with the same flat-arc feel; alternate, 3 sets total. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; effective on a range mat if no chipping green is accessible. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: both balls contacted cleanly on 7 of 10 sets; single-ball pitches noticeably softer-landing. • Competitive: 9 of 10 sets; finesse pitches land and check within 4 feet on a typical firm surface. **Common mistakes.** Balls too close together (no real arc change required); too much forward shaft lean (goal is shallow, not delofted); abandoning the two-ball setup before the flat-arc feel transfers to the single ball.$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact,trajectory}', 'blocked', 'Brech Spradley, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/2-ball-pitching-drill-brech-spradley/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$3-Foot Circle$drill$, $drill$Hole 12 balls in a clock-face circle at 3 feet — including breaking putts from every point — and restart on any miss.$drill$, 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Pelz's research showed PGA Tour players make ~95% of 3-foot putts in competition while 20-handicaps make ~81% — a gap that costs real strokes across 18 holes (Pelz's in-play figures, not PGA Tour ShotLink, which lumps in tap-ins). The clock-face circle addresses why: most players practice 3-footers flat, straight, and from one direction, so a 3-footer that breaks 2 inches or runs downhill — the majority on real courses — has no ingrained reference. Twelve positions around the hole means every putt breaks a different amount: the two fall-line positions (straight up/down) are zero-break anchors, the diagonals are moderate breakers, the two side positions curl the full slope and are hardest. The restart-on-miss rule adds the pressure that makes holing a routine short putt feel familiar under competition. Phil Mickelson credits Hall-of-Famer Jackie Burke with an advanced version — 10 balls around the hole with break, make 100 in a row before leaving — using a 25-back/75-through stroke ratio for forward-rolling momentum. **Who it helps most.** Everyone. Beginners/casual players build baseline reliability and exposure to every break type; developing/competitive players use the restart rule to replicate par-saving pressure. **Setup.** Find the cup's fall line; mark 12 positions at exactly 3 feet, clock-face spaced, 12 o'clock straight uphill, 6 o'clock straight downhill; one ball each. **How.** Start at 6 o'clock and putt counter-clockwise; make all 12, one miss resets to the start; track your best streak. Advanced (Mickelson): 10 balls at 3 feet with gentle break, make 100 consecutive, with Burke's 25/75 tempo. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; a full circuit takes 3–5 minutes if you're holing them, and pressure builds near putt 10–11. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: one full circuit of 12 without a miss at least once per session (8 before a miss is a solid start). • Developing: 3 full circuits; extra reps at the side positions (3 and 9 o'clock). • Competitive: 3 circuits consistently; attempt the Mickelson 100-in-a-row (a realistic short-session target of 50 consecutive). **Common mistakes.** Practicing only the comfortable straight-uphill position while rushing the downhill/side positions (the ones that cost strokes); starting too far back (nail 3 feet first); on the Mickelson version, drifting the stroke longer on both ends instead of 25/75 (a decelerating stroke, left-side misses).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts}', 'pressure_game', 'Dave Pelz (Putting Bible); progression: Phil Mickelson (Secrets of the Short Game)', 'https://synlawngolf.com/partnerships/dave-pelz/golf-practice/elements-of-practice-short-putts/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$5-Target Random Wedge Test$drill$, $drill$Pick five random wedge distances, commit one shot each with a full routine, and score pass/fail — the simplest way to convert range reps into course pressure.$drill$, 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Practical Golf's Ultimate Wedge Drill is built on a behavioral fact: golfers practice the same distance in blocks (ten at 80, ten at 70), but on the course they never get the same shot twice in a row. Block practice builds mechanics, not decision-making or distance-switching feel. Random practice — a different distance every shot — builds course-ready execution that block work never reaches. The "5 questions" structure adds pass/fail accountability: five targets across your wedge range, one shot each with a full routine, 1 point per shot inside your window. Five distances, one shot each, real pressure — a far better simulation of the five or six wedge shots you face in a round than fifty blocks of one distance. **Who it helps most.** Casual, developing, and competitive players who own their stock carries but struggle to execute under course conditions — the "practice well, play poorly" wedge player is almost always over-doing block practice. Beginners benefit from the routine element with adjusted targets. **Setup.** One or two scoring wedges; an open bay with visual markers or GPS; a scoring card. Decide the target window first: 10 yards for casual/developing, 6 for competitive. **How.** Each shot: (1) call/write a specific, randomly chosen distance (e.g., 47, 73, 88, 62, 55 yards); (2) run a full pre-shot routine; (3) hit; (4) score whether it landed in the window. Five shots, five scores; rest 60 seconds; run again with five new distances. Four to six tests per session. 3+/5 is passing for developing players; log session scores — improvement here predicts on-course wedge improvement better than any block metric. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes (4–6 tests, 20–30 shots); run it as the closing segment of any wedge session — block practice to warm up mechanics, random test to convert to skill. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 2–3 of 5 within a 12-yard window; routine on all 5. • Developing: 3–4 of 5 within 10 yards; routine holds under pressure. • Competitive: 4–5 of 5 within 6 yards; logs and tracks weekly. **Common mistakes.** Choosing comfortable distances instead of truly random ones (must include the uncomfortable half- and three-quarter ranges); skipping the routine because it feels slow; doing the test cold with no warm-up and using a bad score as "proof" wedge practice doesn't work; counting shots that roll to the target rather than carry.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,dispersion}', 'pressure_game', 'Practical Golf', 'https://practical-golf.com/the-ultimate-wedge-practice-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Anti-Flip Chip$drill$, $drill$Combine Lorena Ochoa's trail-hand-only chip with Jon Hearn's alignment-stick-to-ribcage drill to eliminate the wrist flip behind chunked and skulled chips.$drill$, 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** The flip — the trail wrist collapsing and the hands scooping through impact — causes the majority of inconsistent chips. These two drills attack the same fault from complementary angles. Ochoa's trail-hand-only chip forces the right wrist to keep its angle because there's no lead hand to mask a collapse — hold the angle and make clean contact, or the ball goes nowhere. Hearn's stick drill then reinforces it with both hands: the stick on your ribcage must not jam your side at impact, which only happens if the body drives the motion rather than the hands flipping. Together they encode a descending, body-led strike. **Who it helps most.** Anyone who chunks or thins chips — the flip is nearly universal in higher handicaps. Developing and competitive players use the Hearn stick drill as a targeted fix, especially if they add speed through the hands under pressure. **Setup.** Phase 1 (Ochoa): ball center, weight slightly left, only the trail hand on the grip, lead arm hanging. Phase 2 (Hearn): normal grip; hold an alignment stick or spare wedge so it points up and rests lightly on the inside of the trail ribcage; front foot slightly open, 70–80% weight forward. (Hearn teaches three ball positions — back armpit for spin, sternum for check, front armpit for release — start at sternum.) **How.** Phase 1: 15 trail-hand chips to a spot ~20 feet away, keeping the right wrist cupped (not collapsed) through impact; clean low contact = angle held; skull/chunk = flip. Phase 2: hip-height swings with the stick — if it jams the ribcage, the body stalled and the hands took over; reset. 10 with the stick, 10 without, alternating until body-drive feels natural. ~15 minutes. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; the Ochoa phase is also a 5-minute pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 10 of 15 trail-hand chips make clean, low contact and travel the intended distance. • Developing: stick drill 10 consecutive without ribcage contact; ball-first on 9 of 10. • Competitive: both phases require no conscious thought — a check when under-pressure flip creeps back. **Common mistakes.** Holding the stick too rigidly (restricts shoulder turn — it rests, not locks); in the Ochoa drill, letting the lead arm push the club through instead of staying out of the way; picking a landing spot too far away, which masks a flip (ball still travels but contact is poor).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact}', 'blocked', 'Lorena Ochoa (Golf Digest); Jon Hearn (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/news/simple-drill-justin-rose-short-game-coach-fixed-chipping-woes/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Ball-First Contact (Towel & Tee)$drill$, $drill$Use a towel behind the ball, a towel in front, or a tee just forward of it to groove the descending, ball-then-turf strike every iron needs.$drill$, 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** Tour pros transfer significant mass to the lead side before impact, which pulls the low point of the swing arc *forward* of the ball and makes a descending, ball-then-turf strike — the divot starts where the ball sat or just ahead. Most recreational players do the opposite: they stall their rotation and try to "help" the ball up, pinning the low point behind the ball, which produces thin or fat contact. Three placement constraints attack that fault and make the fix impossible to fake. The towel behind the ball (HackMotion) is a deterrence cue — bottom out early and you clip it, instant proof the low point is too far back. A second towel in front (Cameron McCormick, Jordan Spieth's longtime coach) is a confirmation cue — you must clip the front towel as the club passes, proving the handle and lead side moved through the zone (McCormick's image: the handle looks like it's over the front towel at impact). The tee in front (Indiana Golf Journal) is a completion cue — a tee pressed in just ahead of the ball must be knocked out, proving the club reached past ball-center on a downward path; if it stays, you scooped. **Who it helps most.** Beginners, casual, and developing players who chronically thin, fat, scoop, or take a divot behind the ball (or none); developing players in a contact regression. Especially useful for players who understand ball-first contact cognitively but can't yet produce it. Not needed for players who already strike ball-before-turf. **Setup.** Grass range or practice tee, a 7 or 8 iron (the steeper iron attack angle is the point — not a wedge, not a wood). Pick one constraint: (a) a dense, folded towel with its near edge ~1.5 clubhead-lengths (2–3 inches) behind the ball; (b) add a second towel ~2 clubhead-lengths in front, ball in the gap; or (c) press a tee into the turf just ahead of the ball, tip at the ball-forward edge, angled slightly toward the target, top just above the turf. **How.** Make a normal full swing, watching the constraint, not ball flight. Towel-behind: a clean miss = low point at/ahead of the ball; clipping it = low point too far back. Two-towel: miss the back, clip the front as the club passes (never reaching the front = the body stalled and the hands flipped). Tee-in-front: tee knocked out = ball-first; tee still standing = you scooped. 10 shots per constraint, then two more sets of 10. Once "miss the back / clip the front / knock the tee" feels automatic, replicate it without looking. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week until 8 of 10 are clean; then a monthly check or when fat/thin/scooping returns. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 5–6 of 10 clean. • Developing: 8 of 10 clear the back (and clip the front / knock the tee). • Competitive: remedial diagnostic only — 10 for 10 before moving on. **Common mistakes.** Towel too far back (passes with a shallow miss); decelerating from fear of the back towel (makes fat contact more likely); slamming down to force the tee out (a dig, not a divot); watching the constraint instead of a normal focus point at impact; starting with a lofted wedge because it's easier (use a 7–8 iron, which exposes the fault); quitting the moment results improve (needs 2–3 weeks to hold under pressure).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{contact}', 'blocked', 'Cameron McCormick (Golf.com); HackMotion; Indiana Golf Journal', 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/cameron-mccormick-tour-pro-iron-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Body-Rotation Wedge (Alignment Stick)$drill$, $drill$Hold an alignment stick as a shaft extension against your lead ribcage to feel whether body rotation — not hand action — powers your wedges.$drill$, 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Justin Rose's wedge philosophy is that distance and contact consistency come from body-driven motion, not hands and wrists manipulating the face. When the body stalls and the hands take over — the most common amateur wedge fault — face angle becomes unpredictable and contact inconsistent. The stick extension makes the fault unmistakable: grip it as an extension of the handle so it runs along your lead forearm and rests against your lead ribcage; make a wedge swing. If the hands flip or the body stops, the stick jams your ribcage; if the body keeps turning with the hands ahead of the ball through impact, the stick clears your side. No interpretation required — it either hits you or it doesn't. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose wedge contact gets inconsistent under pressure, especially those who flip or decelerate on short shots; anyone whose finesse-wedge distance control wanders because the hands are doing the work (same "swing," different distances day to day). **Setup.** Hold an alignment stick against the grip so it extends past the butt along the forearms, lightly touching the lead ribcage at address. Any wedge; start with your most-used scoring wedge. **How.** Half-to-three-quarter swings, focused on the stick clearing the ribcage through impact. Start with slow practice swings (no ball) until five consecutive clear without contact; then a ball — 10 with the stick, 10 without, replicating the clear. Rose progresses from hip-height finesse swings up to fuller wedges in one session. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week in a technique phase; competitive players, 5–10 minutes as a pre-round check when wedge contact feels off. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 7 of 10 stick-attached swings clear the ribcage. • Competitive: 10 of 10 clear; normal shots show a consistent forward-leaning shaft at impact with no flip. **Common mistakes.** Holding the stick loosely so it drifts off the ribcage (nullifies feedback); consciously "keeping the stick away" with tension (counterproductive); swinging only to hip height to avoid the difficulty (drill it at the lengths where the fault appears); ignoring a jab and continuing — stop, reset, find the cause.$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{contact,distance_control}', 'blocked', 'Justin Rose (Golf Digest)', 'https://golfdigest.com/story/justin-rose-how-to-control-your-wedges', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Bunker No-Ball Splash$drill$, $drill$Make 10 aggressive splash swings without a ball to build sand-contact feel and commitment tempo before your first real bunker shot.$drill$, 5, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Bunker shots fail more from hesitation than mechanics. The distinctive feel of the club skimming through sand — the resistance, the forward explosion, the high follow-through — is hard to learn for the first time under pressure. No-ball splash swings remove the anxiety of a result and let the body repeat the correct club-through-sand sensation quickly. PGA teaching stresses practicing "until it feels natural," because a bunker shot is fundamentally different from every other shot: you're hitting the sand under the ball, not the ball. Without a ball to fixate on, that intent gets clearer and the swing more committed. **Who it helps most.** All levels equally. Beginners learn the motion safely without a defiant ball sitting in the sand; casual/developing players restore feel after time away; competitive players use it as a mandatory pre-round warm-up — the first 10 swings in any bunker should be no-ball to read how the sand plays that day. **Setup.** Open the sand- or lob-wedge face (slightly skyward); open the stance; twist your feet a half-inch in to gauge depth; address a patch of sand as if a ball sat there; draw a small entry line 1–2 inches behind the imaginary ball. **How.** Make 10 full, committed splash swings — not tentative pokes. Enter at the line and continue through, the bounce skimming forward; sand should spray toward the target. A tidy forward explosion = correct entry and depth. If the club catches and stops, you led with the edge and decelerated — reset with more speed and a more open face. After 10, assess: soft/fluffy sand (slower, less sand) or firm (more speed, shallower entry). **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes, always the first activity in any bunker session; on the course, 5–8 no-ball splashes before your first ball is the highest-value bunker warm-up available. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 7 of 10 produce a forward-moving sand explosion. • Casual: 8 of 10 consistent in shape and depth. • Developing: 10 of 10 controlled splashes; a verbal read on the sand before the first ball. • Competitive: automatic; dialing exact sand speed for the day. **Common mistakes.** Tentative half-speed swings that bury the club (commit and accelerate); entering too far behind the imaginary ball (too-heavy splash); not opening the face enough so the edge catches and the club stops.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,tempo}', 'warmup', 'PGA.com', 'https://pga.com/story/how-to-hit-any-bunker-shot', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Bunker Sand-Entry$drill$, $drill$Draw a line in the sand and drop a dollar bill on it to train the precise sand-entry point — 1–2 inches behind the ball — that every consistent bunker shot needs.$drill$, 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** The two common bunker failures — skulls over the green and chunks that stay in the sand — both trace to an inconsistent sand-entry point. GOLFTEC's Line Exercise establishes a visual reference: a line drawn perpendicular to the target, one to two inches behind the ball, with the goal of equal sand displaced on both sides after impact (a centered, consistent entry). Brech Spradley's dollar-bill variation adds tactile feedback without a ball: a bill in the sand flutters out toward the target if the bounce and entry point are right, but stays buried or shoots sideways if the leading edge digs or the entry is too far back. Visual plus physical confirmation of proper technique. **Who it helps most.** All levels — bunker play is undertrained at every handicap. Beginners learn the foundational entry point; casual/developing players recalibrate a fat- or thin-entry habit; competitive players run the line drill before competition to confirm bounce and entry on that day's sand. **Setup.** Open the sand-wedge face slightly (pointing a touch skyward); open the stance left of target (righty); draw a line across the target line; place a dollar bill (or folded paper) centered on it, pressed lightly into the sand, with a ball 1–2 inches in front. For the GOLFTEC no-ball version, omit the ball first. **How.** Phase 1 (no ball): 5 practice swings bottoming out exactly on the line — not behind, not ahead — checking for equal sand on both sides. Phase 2 (bill + ball): swing with the same entry focus; the bill should flutter out toward the target as the ball pops out soft. Bill buried = too steep or too far back. 10 shots with the bill, 10 without, carrying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week; a 5-minute pre-round warm-up whenever a practice bunker's available. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 6 of 10 bill shots flutter the bill toward target; ball exits consistently. • Casual: 7 of 10; feeling how sand conditions change required speed. • Developing: 9 of 10; can adjust entry and speed for firm vs soft sand. • Competitive: all 10; line drill used only to calibrate unusual sand. **Common mistakes.** Drawing the line too far behind the ball (heavy, short shots); too little face-open so the leading edge leads instead of the bounce (the face should feel almost uncomfortably open); decelerating — you must accelerate, as sand absorbs speed.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker}', 'blocked', 'GOLFTEC; Brech Spradley (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/bunker-shots/100-bill-bunker-drill-brech-spradley/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Confidence-Builder (Yarwood Hole-Feel)$drill$, $drill$Make the same 4-foot putt five different ways to rewire your brain into seeing the hole as bigger than it feels.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** The 4.25-inch cup fits more than two golf balls side by side. Most amateurs who flinch on short putts don't flinch because the hole is small — they've mentally shrunk it to a keyhole by fixating on one "correct" entry. GOLF Top 100 Teacher Jonathan Yarwood's drill is a psychological reset: repeated makes with varied entry points prove the cup accepts a wide range of speeds and angles. Yarwood describes it as "psychologically allowing you to understand that ball goes in a lot of ways" — once you've watched it fall through the left edge, the right edge, the back, and at dying pace in one session, the association shifts and the hole looks bigger. Perceived target size genuinely correlates with confidence and performance under pressure. **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players building a foundation; developing players with a sound stroke missing short putts under pressure from tightening. Competitive players doing full pressure training get more from the 3-Foot Circle — this drill is about confidence repair and perceptual expansion. **Setup.** One cup on a flat or mildly sloped surface at ~4 feet; at least 5 balls; no alignment aids — this is feel and intention, not mechanical tracking. **How.** Roll five putts targeting five entry types: (1) center, perfect pace; (2) center, dying pace, toppling in on the last revolution; (3) center, firm into the back of the cup; (4) right edge — aim at the right side and fall in off the right lip; (5) left edge. Each made putt reinforces a pathway. Miss one, don't reset — repeat that entry until holed, then continue. Cover all five, then repeat the sequence 2–3 times. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week during short-putt yips or confidence loss; a good pre-round alternative to high-pressure gate work. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: complete all five entry types at least once per session. • Developing: two consecutive full sequences (10 putts, all five types twice) without missing any attempt. • Competitive: three quick sequences as a reset; move to the Circle Drill for pressure reps. **Common mistakes.** Cheating the "dying speed" entry by hitting it firmly (the point is the slowest possible make); using a gate/aid (turns a psychological exercise into a mechanical one); treating a miss as a crisis instead of noting the entry type and repeating until made.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{short_putts}', 'pressure_game', 'Jonathan Yarwood, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/tour-proven-drill-improve-putting-play-smart/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Distance Ladder$drill$, $drill$Putt through a graduated ladder of distances with a scoring system that rewards smart pace, not just made putts.$drill$, 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Three-putts rarely happen because you can't make a 30-footer; they happen because the first putt finished 8 feet past or 6 short. Distance ladders train the skill that prevents them: controlling speed across a range of distances under mild scoring pressure. Three sources give the same progressive architecture with useful variations — the Golf.com ladder uses birdie/par/bogey scoring for urgency; Tessa Teachman's Leapfrog (SuperStroke) pushes each ball just past the previous for pure increment feel; PGA Tour player Sam Ryder uses two coins as a finish zone across five distances, restarting on any miss to build commitment to pace over direction. **Who it helps most.** Casual through developing players who three-putt from 20–40 feet (the scoring pressure is the value); competitive players use the restart-on-miss variant as a tournament-stakes simulation. **Setup.** Golf.com format: a tee one pace (~3 feet) behind the hole; balls at 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 feet. Ryder format: two coins 2.5 feet apart centered on the hole; tees at 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 feet. Leapfrog: a start tee and a 15-foot boundary; no hole. **How.** Golf.com: roll the five in distance order — holed = birdie (+1), finishing between cup and tee = par (0), short or hitting the tee = bogey (−1); goal 1-under across five (step each ball back two paces to push the longest to ~30 feet for more difficulty). Ryder: from 15 feet, stop a ball between the coins; advance through 20–35; any putt outside the coins resets to 15. Leapfrog: each ball lands past the previous one until one lands short; beat your streak. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; pair one Ryder run (precision under pressure) with one Leapfrog run (incremental feel). **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: even or better in the Golf.com format at 3–15 feet; reach ball 4 in Leapfrog without backtracking. • Developing: 1-under in Golf.com; complete the Ryder ladder 15–35 with no restart. • Competitive: 2-under on the extended ladder; complete the Ryder ladder twice without a reset. **Common mistakes.** Treating it as make-or-miss (the finish zone, not the hole, is the long-putt target — a 2-foot-past lip-out is a failure); only using downhill putts (skews feel firm; mix slopes so uphill pace develops too).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game', 'Golf.com; Tessa Teachman/SuperStroke; Sam Ryder (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/use-ladder-drill-roll-putts-like-pga-tour-pro/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$DJ Wedge Combine$drill$, $drill$Hit half, three-quarter, and full shots with each wedge and demand five straight carries inside a tight yardage window — Dustin Johnson's pre-round tightening protocol.$drill$, 30, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Dustin Johnson sat just inside the top 40 in proximity from inside 100 yards before overhauling his practice; after adopting a combine-style, launch-monitor-tracked approach he climbed to third on Tour in that stat within a year. The combine format works because it applies a pass/fail performance window: set a window, hit until you achieve it, move on — unlike "hitting a bucket of wedges" with no criteria and no pressure. The five-within-a-window target also forces you to notice dispersion, not just average carry — a single outlier that blows the window flags a sequencing or tempo issue. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with established wedge technique who want to convert known carries into repeatable execution under self-imposed pressure. Needs at least estimated carry data; a GPS range or launch monitor helps. **Setup.** Three wedges (gap, 56°, 60°). Launch monitor ideal; else a range with known yardages and visual estimation. Window: 5 yards for developing, 3 for competitive. **How.** Lob wedge first: half swings until five consecutive carries fall within your window; record average. Then three-quarter, then full. Move to sand, then gap — same three lengths each. On an outlier, reset the *window counter* (not the whole set) and continue. ~45–54 shots; experienced players finish in 30 minutes. **Time & frequency.** 25–35 minutes; pre-round once a week or before any competitive start; one wedge's three lengths (~10 min) on off-days to maintain calibration. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: all nine sets (3 wedges × 3 lengths) with five-ball windows ≤8 yards. • Competitive: windows ≤5 yards for all nine; knows each carry within 2 yards without a monitor. **Common mistakes.** Resetting the whole set instead of the window counter; making compensatory swings to fill a window rather than diagnosing the outlier; skipping half-swing sets (those 40–60-yard shots are the highest-leverage scoring range).$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'pressure_game', 'GolfWRX; Golf.com', 'https://golfwrx.com/509476/improve-your-wedge-distance-control-with-dustin-johnsons-unique-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Driver Dispersion Mapping$drill$, $drill$Plot all 20 of your drives — including the bad ones — to build a true dispersion picture that should inform every tee-shot decision you make.$drill$, 25, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Jon Sherman's core argument: most golfers manage tee shots based on a fictional "when I hit it well" trajectory. Real course management needs the actual distribution — where drives land across a representative 20-shot sample, blow-ups included. The mapping protocol produces that: a scatter that reveals whether your dispersion is symmetric or biased, how wide your 1-sigma corridor really is, and whether your miss is a predictable working shot or a low-frequency disaster. A player who knows their dispersion is 30 yards wide with a slight right bias can aim 10 yards left of center on a 40-yard fairway and never miss left. Without the map they aim center and lose half their drives right. **Who it helps most.** Competitive players (break-80 to scratch) leaking shots to course-management errors rather than swing errors. It's a measurement exercise — it presupposes enough consistency that 20 shots are a pattern, not chaos. Not for beginners/casual players whose dispersion is too large to be actionable. **Setup.** Range with yardage + lateral markers, or a launch monitor/sim; a printed grid or notes app. Fix one aim line for all 20 shots; full routine on every ball. **How.** Commit to one aim line for all 20. Hit each with full effort and routine — no do-overs. Record each: carry, and left/right deviation from the aim line in yards. After 20, find (1) your 1-sigma band width (the middle ~14 shots); (2) your miss tendency (average deviation; positive = right bias); (3) your blow-up rate (shots beyond 2× the typical miss). Repeat monthly or before a big round. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, once a month as a benchmark — not weekly. The training response goes into the Fairway Target Game or path work. **Success metric, by level.** • Break 80: 1-sigma corridor ≤30 yards; an identifiable (non-random) miss pattern. • Break 70s: corridor ≤20 yards; miss tendency within 5 yards of the aim line. • Scratch: corridor ≤15 yards; blow-ups (beyond 30 yards offline) under 10%. **Common mistakes.** Changing the aim line mid-session (invalidates the data); excluding bad shots (the blow-ups are the most valuable data); mapping once and never again (dispersion shifts with technique); using the data to justify aggressive lines rather than to calibrate conservative ones (Sherman's point: it should make you more conservative).$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,course_management}', 'skill_game', 'Jon Sherman (Practical Golf)', 'https://practical-golf.com/driver-dispersion', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Eyes-Closed Speed Calibration$drill$, $drill$Stroke a putt with eyes closed, call your shot before you open them, and learn to trust kinesthetic feel over visual anxiety.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Three-time major champion Padraig Harrington uses this to solve a persistent problem: watching the putter head during the stroke rather than committing to the feel of the swing. When you can see the putter, your nervous system reacts to perceived mis-hits in real time — a twitch, a last-millisecond adjustment — and those micro-corrections turn a smooth stroke jerky. Eyes closed eliminates that loop: "if you can't see the putter head, you can't react to it." It also calibrates distance — hitting several 25–30-footers eyes-closed and predicting short/long builds an internal speed model for the day's pace (9-stimp greens feel different from 11). Some elite players have used the technique in actual competition, underscoring how deeply feel-over-sight underlies consistent putting. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with sound strokes that deteriorate under performance anxiety (the protocol disrupts the visual-reactive flinch); casual players who over-steer. Less useful for complete beginners who still need visual feedback for basic path and face awareness. **Setup.** Any putt 15–30 feet, flat or mildly breaking; no equipment; one ball per cycle. **How.** Step 1: roll a normal open-eyes putt from ~30 feet; note where it finishes. Step 2: a second ball, same distance — take your read and commit with eyes open, then close your eyes just before the backstroke and keep them closed through impact and 2 seconds after. Step 3: before opening, call it — solid strike? left or right? short or long? Step 4: open and compare. Prediction accuracy is the metric, not whether it went in. 5–8 cycles per session, occasionally from 10–15 feet. Pre-round: 4–6 eyes-closed putts from 20–30 feet to calibrate the day's speed (Harrington explicitly recommends this). **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week in technical phases; 5 minutes in every pre-round warm-up as a speed station. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: prediction matches reality (short/long, left/right) on 5 of 8. • Developing: 6–7 of 8; regularly senses heel/toe contact without seeing it. • Competitive: 7–8 of 8; can reproduce a target speed ("2 feet past") to within 18 inches on command. **Common mistakes.** Opening the eyes at impact because it feels wrong (keep them closed a full 2 seconds after); judging by hole-outs (a made putt with a wrong prediction teaches nothing; an accurate read on a miss teaches everything); skipping the prediction step (turns training into a novelty).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,tempo}', 'skill_game', 'Padraig Harrington (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/padraig-harrington-eyes-closed-putting-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Fairway Target Game$drill$, $drill$Simulate a round of tee shots to a virtual fairway that shrinks as you improve — competitive pressure without leaving the range.$drill$, 20, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Jon Sherman's Practical Golf research and MassGolf's range games reach the same insight: practicing driver by swinging freely and watching shape rarely improves on-course dispersion, because the range has no consequences. The fairway game adds a scoring consequence and a shrinking target mirroring a real tee shot — a fairway is ~30–40 yards wide and your driver must fit. Sherman's data shows most casual golfers have 40+ yard dispersion and don't know it because they never measure. The game makes dispersion visible under pressure; the MassGolf shrinking variant adds a progression (hit it three straight, it narrows). **Who it helps most.** Casual players with no reference for acceptable dispersion; developing players who know they must keep it in play but never trained to a corridor; competitive players use a tighter starting fairway (25 yards) for pressure. All benefit from the one-shot-per-hole commitment. **Setup.** Range targets/markers at driver distance, or set the fairway width on a sim. If no markers, use two sticks or landmarks (flags, buckets) to define a 40-yard corridor at ~220–250 yards. Score: fairway = 1, miss = 0; record every shot. **How.** Play 9 "holes." Each: pick a specific aim line (vary it to simulate doglegs), run your full routine, hit one driver, record hit/miss and miss side. Tally after 9. Hit 7 of 9 → narrow the corridor 5 yards next round; under 5 of 9 → keep it until you hit 7+. Track miss patterns: same side every time is path/face information. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes (~18 tee shots + routines + recording), once a week as an assessment; add sessions during a driving-accuracy block. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 5 of 9 in a 40-yard corridor consistently. • Developing: 7 of 9 in 35 yards; miss pattern moving from one-sided to random. • Competitive: 7 of 9 in 25 yards; can simulate course shapes (aim at the right edge to play a fade to center). **Common mistakes.** Skipping the routine (turns it back into range-bashing); not tracking miss side (the direction is half the picture); too-generous corridor (no improvement signal); multiple balls per "hole" (one-and-done is what golf asks).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'skill_game', 'MassGolf; Jon Sherman (Practical Golf)', 'https://practical-golf.com/driver-dispersion', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Gate Drill$drill$, $drill$Roll putts through a narrow tee gate to groove a square face and a pure start line.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** On a putt, face angle at impact controls the large majority of the ball's starting direction — putting-launch research commonly cites ~83%, up to ~90% on a center strike — while path barely matters at short range. Most missed short putts aren't misreads; they're tiny face errors, a degree or two, invisible at address. The gate makes that error unmissable: an off-square face clips a tee. Binary feedback on the single thing that matters most inside 10 feet, a staple from David Leadbetter to tour coaches. **Who it helps most.** Every level benefits, but developing and competitive players already rolling a reasonable stroke get the most — the gate exposes the last 1–2°. Beginners use a slightly wider gate as a habit-builder. **Setup.** Flat, straight 5–10 ft putt; two tees just outside toe and heel, a few mm clearance each side (wider for higher handicaps); ball midway; optional second gate ahead to check start line. **How.** Normal stroke, roll through; clean pass = square face + center strike; a clipped tee shows the miss direction; sets of ten, reset the count on any miss. **Time & frequency.** 5–10 min, 2–3×/week; also a sharp pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 6–7 of 10 clean at 5 ft. • Developing: 8–9 of 10 at 6–8 ft. • Competitive: 10 in a row, then back up to 10 ft. **Common mistakes.** A gate so wide it never gives feedback; steering the stroke to dodge the tees; staring at the gate instead of the hole.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,contact}', 'blocked', 'David Leadbetter / Titleist / HackMotion', 'https://hackmotion.com/gate-putting-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Harrington Weight-Forward Chip$drill$, $drill$Stake an alignment stick just inside your front heel so you can never shift your weight back through impact — Harrington's cure for chunked chips.$drill$, 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** The most common cause of chunked chips is what Harrington identifies as moving your center of gravity backward in the downswing — the body instinctively trying to help the ball up. The club then bottoms out behind the ball. A stick in the ground just inside the front heel creates a consequence for that shift: touch it and you know you moved back. The immediate, undeniable feedback corrects the pattern faster than any swing thought, and it builds the "sitting on the left side" sensation Harrington calls the foundation of ball-first contact. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who chunk chips, especially those who feel the urge to help the ball up or were taught to keep weight even. Players returning from a break recalibrate forward-weight patterns quickly with it. **Setup.** On a chipping area, plant an alignment stick vertically (3–4 inches up) just inside the front heel — close enough to touch your leg if your weight moves back, but not touching at address. Normal chip address, ball slightly behind center, hands ahead. **How.** Chip with the sole goal of never touching the stick. Harrington's cue: "sit" on the lead side — lead hip quiet, weight pressing into the front foot throughout, hands ahead of the ball at impact, front side loaded (not rising). 10 chips per set, reset, 3 sets. At home: front heel on carpet, take practice divots on the carpet — same pattern, no stick. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week; especially effective in the first 10 minutes of a short-game session before target practice. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: completes a 10-chip set with no stick contact on at least 7 swings; contact noticeably cleaner. • Developing: 10 consecutive with no contact; ball-first on all 10. **Common mistakes.** Setting the stick so far from the foot it never gets touched (must actually challenge); over-leaning so far left the spine angle changes and contact goes thin; watching the stick instead of the ball at impact.$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,contact}', 'blocked', 'Padraig Harrington (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/drill-padraig-harrington-consistent-chipping/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Headcover Path$drill$, $drill$Set a headcover just outside the toe at address to train an inside path and kill the over-the-top move behind pulls and slices.$drill$, 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Jonathan Yarwood's Titleist drill builds a physical gate that makes an over-the-top, outside-in path impossible without hitting the headcover. A pull or slice with the driver is almost always path: the club approaches from outside the line and glances across the ball, creating sidespin. The headcover sits exactly where an outside-in clubhead would travel; hitting it is instant, unambiguous punishment. The correct inside-to-square path threads the clubhead inside the headcover, past the ball, and out to the right of target early in the follow-through. It teaches path without an abstract plane image and works for visual learners (they see the obstacle) and proprioceptive learners (they feel the change). **Who it helps most.** Developing players with a consistent slice or pull see immediate feedback; competitive players use it to correct an over-the-top pattern that creeps in under pressure and as a pre-round shallowing drill. **Setup.** Range, driver, one headcover (a small iron headcover is less intimidating than a driver one). Tee normally; place the headcover ~6 inches outside the toe (right of the head for a righty), level with the ball, on the ground or a low tee. Larger gap for beginners to the drill, tighten as path improves. **How.** Address with the headcover in place; full swing. Untouched = neutral-to-inside path; struck = outside-in. Don't steer around it — make full, committed swings and adjust by outcome. Thought: trace the inside of a barrel from top to impact. 3 rehearsal swings without a ball, then 10 balls; two sets. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on path; pre-round (3–5 swings, then remove) is a competitive standard. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: headcover untouched on 7 of 10; flight moving from pull/slice to straight or slight draw. • Competitive: untouched every swing; normal shape maintained. **Common mistakes.** Headcover too far outside (room to be outside-in and miss it); casting early to get inside (clears it but produces fat contact); gripping tighter from fear of hitting it; using a bulky driver headcover that creates needless fear.$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'blocked', 'Jonathan Yarwood (Titleist)', 'https://titleist.com/videos/instruction/driver-headcover-drill', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Hinge & Hold$drill$, $drill$Break the wrists sharply going back and hold the angle through impact — Phil Mickelson's method for consistent, spin-controlling chips.$drill$, 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Mickelson argues in Secrets of the Short Game that hinge-and-hold is the fundamentally sound chipping technique because it produces a descending blow, creates backspin, and allows precise speed control. The "hinge" — an immediate wrist break going back — sets a steep angle that ensures a downward path and ball-before-turf contact. The "hold" — keeping that wrist angle through and past impact — prevents the scoop. Mickelson is explicit: be aggressive into and through the ball. The held angle plus a short, accelerating follow-through is what makes the ball check rather than run out, giving control on tight pins and firm greens. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already control ball-first contact and want to add trajectory and spin control. Not a beginner drill — the steep angle causes mis-hits without a contact foundation. **Setup.** A 60° lob wedge from just off the green (Mickelson's most-shown lie). Ball back of center, 60–70% weight forward, hands pressed ahead — a compact address, not reaching. **How.** Take the club back with an immediate, deliberate hinge (clubhead near vertical by the time hands are waist height) — the hinge begins the moment the club moves, not late. Then drive the body aggressively through while holding the wrist angle; the follow-through is short and low, exiting left rather than swinging up. Drill: 5 chips to a spot, confirm the ball checks, move the target back 5 feet, repeat. 3 sets of 5 per distance, 3 distances. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; also the first few chips of any short-game session to activate the hinge feel. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean contact and a noticeable check at landing on 6 of 10. • Competitive: 9 of 10 land within 3 feet of the intended landing zone and check to within 5 feet of the hole from 20 yards. **Common mistakes.** Hinging late (one-piece takeaway then a flick — loses the steep angle); holding the angle but decelerating (the hold only works if you accelerate; deceleration = fat); using it on 40-yard pitches (most effective on greenside chips).$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact,trajectory}', 'blocked', 'Phil Mickelson, Secrets of the Short Game (video)', NULL, 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Know Your Iron Distances$drill$, $drill$Hit ten carry-focused shots with every iron, log the averages, and build the personal yardage card that makes every approach a known quantity.$drill$, 45, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Digest's iron-distance protocol rests on one insight: your real *average* carry, not your *best* carry, is what matters. Most amateurs carry a mental yardage 10–15 yards optimistic because it came from their best shot — the one they hit once in six. That optimism drives chronic short-siding and back-of-green misses. The fix is simple but uncomfortable: hit 10 balls per club under no-pressure range conditions, discard obvious mishits, and average the rest. A proper gapping session also reveals overlaps (two clubs within 5 yards) and gaps (a 20-yard jump), both of which affect equipment and strategy. **Who it helps most.** Every level, but the relative gain is highest for beginners and casual players playing with completely wrong assumptions. Developing players often know their scoring irons but guess on long irons. Competitive players use it as post-fitting verification and seasonal recalibration. **Setup.** A range with measured flags or a GPS/launch monitor; a notepad/phone; your full iron set from shortest scoring wedge to longest iron/hybrid. Plan 45 minutes: ~10 balls/club, 7–8 clubs, plus rest. **How.** Start with the gap or pitching wedge: 10 full, committed, *normal* stock swings (not your hardest). Note each carry, ignoring roll. Discard the two outliers (one thin, one flushed), average the remaining 8 — that's your carry. Move up a club; repeat. Build a table (club/carry/total). Gaps >15 yards suggest a loft gap or missing hybrid; overlaps <8 yards mean a redundant club. Write the carries on a card; reference it before every approach until automatic. **Time & frequency.** 45 minutes for a full 7-club session; once each season start, after gear or swing changes, and after a multi-week break (cold air cuts carry 5–8%). **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: a written card for at least 5 clubs, used instead of guessing. • Developing: full 7-club table within 5 yards of accurate; can recite each carry. • Competitive: full bag mapped incl. wedge swing lengths; recalibrated after conditions/gear; no club-selection uncertainty 60–180 yards. **Common mistakes.** Swinging your hardest to maximize the number (unreproducible on course); counting total distance (incl. roll) instead of carry; using range balls without noting they fly 5–10% shorter; doing it tired/rushed (skews the average); relying on memory instead of logging (drifts optimistic within two weeks).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'warmup', 'Golf Digest', 'https://golfdigest.com/story/golf-digest-how-to-ways-to-dial-your-iron-distances', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Nine Windows$drill$, $drill$Work through all nine combinations of three trajectories and three shot shapes with one iron to build complete ball-flight command.$drill$, 20, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods made this the backbone of his iron warm-up because it forces complete ownership of ball flight rather than grooving one shape. The matrix — three trajectory rows (low/medium/high) across three shape columns (draw/straight/fade) — equals nine windows. Working opposite corners (e.g., high draw to low fade) keeps the swing balanced and prevents over-practicing one compensation. As Woods put it: "It is three different trajectories... Then... whether you are going to cut them or draw them. Those are your nine windows." On the course you choose the window the situation demands — the slope behind the pin, the wind, the miss you can't afford — so practicing all nine means none is foreign under pressure. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with functional iron mechanics who want to expand shot-making. Not a contact drill — it presupposes consistent center-face contact. Competitive players use it as a warm-up protocol; developing players use it to find which windows expose technique gaps. **Setup.** One mid-iron (6 or 7); a target 150–180 yards out; a 3×3 tic-tac-toe grid on paper to track windows. **How.** Work across the matrix or corner to corner. Low: ball half-back, stand slightly closer (steeper, de-lofted). High: ball forward, hands finish high. Draw: face closed to path, full rotation through. Fade: face slightly open, soft hands through. One shot per window, tracked on the grid; note which needed the most attempts — Woods says those reveal your current limitation, so concentrate follow-up there. One pass = 9 shots; a full warm-up is 2–3 passes (18–27). **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3 passes; a pre-round warm-up for competitive rounds. Off-season, a dedicated session to build new windows; low fade and high draw are typically hardest for righties. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: all nine hit with intentional shape (deliberate, not perfect). • Competitive: all nine matching intent within a club-width of dispersion; can identify and execute the window the situation demands. **Common mistakes.** Only hitting your natural shape at different heights instead of truly changing shape; treating each window as a separate swing rather than small setup levers (ball position, stance, finish height); wrong iron (a wedge makes trajectory windows too hard to read; a 3-iron makes everything a battle); skipping uncomfortable windows instead of using them as diagnostics.$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,dispersion,start_line}', 'skill_game', 'Tiger Woods (GolfWRX/TaylorMade)', 'https://golfwrx.com/664080/tiger-woods-breaks-down-his-famous-nine-window-warm-up-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$One-Handed Stroke$drill$, $drill$Putt trail-hand-only then lead-hand-only to develop the independent feel that creates topspin, tempo, and a shorter stroke.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods uses trail-hand-only putting as a pre-round warm-up, and GOLF Top 100 Teacher Scott Munroe explains the mechanics: the trail hand (right, for righties) is the primary feel and speed hand in most strokes. Isolating it lets the putter toe release naturally through impact — important for toe-hang designs — generating true topspin rather than a skid. Topspin produces predictable roll; skid produces unpredictable early bounce, which is why 20-footers run out differently than expected. Munroe's insight: "Speed control comes from good spin, and good spin allows you to have a shorter stroke — a shorter stroke is always better, as less can go wrong." One-handed practice makes a sloppy two-handed stroke physically impossible. The lead-hand-only version builds the resistance half — the pull that keeps the through-stroke from accelerating uncontrollably. **Who it helps most.** Developing players with yippy or jerky tempo; competitive players dialing touch and topspin before a round. Casual players can use it but may find the trail-hand version frustrating at first — start at 3–4 feet before lag distances. **Setup.** A flat green with 10–15 feet of space; your gamer putter (the toe-release benefit is most pronounced with toe-hang, but any putter works). **How.** Trail-hand only: trail hand mid-grip, lead hand behind your back; start at 3–5 feet feeling the toe release past the heel; work back to 8–10 feet; 10 putts. Lead-hand only: lead hand only, trail behind the back — this half is control, the lead hand pulling without overpowering; 10 putts from 6–10 feet. Finish two-handed: the stroke should feel smoother and lower-effort. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week; a great pre-round touch warm-up. Keep it short — fatigue defeats a feel drill. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 10 trail-hand putts from 4 feet without visible skid. • Developing: 8 of 10 trail-hand from 6–8 feet with consistent toe-release; 7 of 10 lead-hand from the same. • Competitive: a 10-putt trail-hand run from 10–12 feet, then reproduce the pace two-handed with no speed loss. **Common mistakes.** Gripping tightly with the single hand (kills feel — let it swing); attempting lag putts before establishing short-distance feel; skipping the two-handed integration putts (negates the transfer).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked', 'Tiger Woods / Scott Munroe, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/tiger-woods-one-handed-putt-practice-strategy/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Path Gate$drill$, $drill$Plant two alignment sticks in a narrow channel and swing through them to enforce a neutral path and square face — no forgiveness for sloppy entry angles.$drill$, 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** The path gate turns swing-path feedback from abstract (plane diagrams) into a physical binary: thread the needle or clip a stick. PGA.com and MyGolfSpy both favor stick gates over mirror or video work because the consequence is immediate and in your periphery during the swing, not reviewed afterward. The gate works on two mechanisms at once: it constrains the clubhead's entry angle (path) and requires a square face to pass cleanly through the narrow opening. A wide gate catches only the worst swings; a tight gate punishes 2–3° of path deviation — the precision that separates 15-handicap dispersion from 5-handicap dispersion with a driver. **Who it helps most.** Developing players with variable path (sometimes inside-out, sometimes over-the-top) who can't self-diagnose; competitive players use a tighter gate as a precision tool and a tour warm-up staple. Not for beginners — the gate needs consistent contact to give useful feedback. **Setup.** Two alignment sticks (in the ground or mat grooves) forming a 6–8-inch channel parallel to the target line, positioned so the impact zone passes through the center — one stick just outside the toe-line, one just inside the heel-line, at or near ground level. Tee the ball in the gate center; narrow as accuracy improves. **How.** Address inside the gate; full swing. Clean pass = path on-line and face square enough; a toe-side clip = outside-in; a heel-side clip = an over-aggressive inside-out. 5 slow-motion practice swings (no ball) to confirm you can pass it, then 10 balls at full speed. Note which side you clip — the pattern reveals your tendency. Alternate 1 gate swing with 1 normal swing. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week when path is the focus; afterward use periodically as a check. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: gate untouched on 6 of 10 full-speed swings; clipping only one side (a consistent tendency to fix). • Competitive: untouched on 9 of 10 at a tight 5-inch setting; shape matches intent. **Common mistakes.** Gate too wide (misses the 3–4° errors that cost 20 yards offline); aiming the gate left of target (makes a correct path look outside-in); steering through instead of committing; not alternating with free swings (the gate becomes a crutch).$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,dispersion}', 'blocked', 'PGA.com; MyGolfSpy', 'https://pga.com/story/the-3-best-alignment-stick-drills-to-play-better-golf', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Pre-Round Putting Warm-Up$drill$, $drill$A structured three-station warm-up that primes lag speed, break reads, and short-putt confidence before your first tee shot.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Cold putting is the most predictable way to three-putt the first hole. Green speed varies course to course and day to day with moisture and mow height, so a stroke calibrated to your home green is wrong everywhere else. GOLF Top 100 Teacher Michael Hunt splits the warm-up into three purposeful stations — distance feel, break reading, and the confidence of watching putts drop. Ten minutes primes the visual-motor loop before you've hit a tee shot, so your stroke isn't running a deficit for the first three holes. **Who it helps most.** Every level, but high-handicappers who rush to the range and skip the green gain the most — they reach the first green cold to speed and line. Competitive players use it as final calibration, not discovery. **Setup.** Putter and a handful of balls. A green with (1) a long flat area for lag, (2) a 12–15-foot putt with visible break, (3) a straight 3–5-foot putt. **How.** Station 1 (lag): 3–6 balls from the longest putt, focus on speed only — no target hole; lengthen the stroke and calibrate to today's pace. Station 2 (break read): from 12–15 feet, place a tee at your read's apex and roll the ball at the tee, confirming the read and that pace reaches the apex; 4–5 attempts. Station 3 (short): on a straight 3-footer (low handicaps step to 4–5 feet), set a club on the target line and roll putts until you've watched 5–6 drop — like a free-throw shooter finding rhythm; end on a make. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes before every round; a pre-round protocol, not a standalone session. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: complete all three stations without rushing; end on a made short putt. • Developing: station-2 read on the correct side on 3 of 5; short putts 100% from 3 feet. • Competitive: read accurate AND pace within a foot of the tee on 4 of 5; a clean run of 5 from 4–5 feet before leaving. **Common mistakes.** Spending all 10 minutes holing 3-footers (lag is where rounds bleed strokes); rushing station 1 with three lazy lag putts; using a featureless lie for station 2 that gives no read feedback.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{lag,short_putts,green_reading}', 'warmup', 'Michael Hunt, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/three-drills-dial-in-putting-weekend-warmup/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Pump & Pause Sequencing$drill$, $drill$Interrupt the downswing at the halfway point once or twice before completing the swing to feel correct transition sequence and prevent early release.$drill$, 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** Butch Harmon's pump drill addresses a top iron-consistency killer: casting, where the wrists unhinge and the clubhead overtakes the hands before impact. The fix makes transition a conscious, interruptible process — take the club to the top, start down, stop when the hands reach halfway, pump back to the top, pause again, then complete and hit. You experience the correct sequence in slow motion: lower body leads, arms drop from the inside, the club lags. Tiger Woods used a related stop-start method (pausing at the top and halfway down) to feel the same check. All versions isolate transition and make casting mechanically impossible during the paused reps. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who go over the top, cast early, or lose lag before impact; players with alternating thins and fats (a sequencing problem). Competitive players rarely need it unless recovering from a swing change. **Setup.** Mid-iron (7 or 8) on grass or mat; a face-on mirror or video helps verify the halfway-down position (hands at hip height, shaft roughly parallel). **How.** Normal address; full backswing; start down and freeze when hands reach hip height; hold one second; pump back to the top; pause again at halfway; then swing through and hit. That's the two-pump version. Alternative single-pause: a three-quarter backswing, pause one second, complete and hit. 10 drill reps, then 10 normal shots retaining the lag/sequence feel; alternate 10/10/10/10. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week in a contact phase; then normal practice with occasional pump sets as a reset. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: contact improves from mostly thin/fat to majority center-face within three sessions. • Developing: can pump twice, hold each pause, and still strike cleanly; normal shots show a divot in front of the ball. **Common mistakes.** Rushing the pause because it feels unnatural (the discomfort is the point); pausing too low near impact (no lag left to preserve); using a driver (the descending sequence is iron/wedge-specific); pumping as fast as a normal swing (kills the slow-motion benefit).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80}', '{contact,tempo}', 'blocked', 'Butch Harmon (Golf Digest); Tiger Woods (Golf365)', 'https://golfdigest.com/story/improve-your-iron-consistency-with-one-of-butch-harmons-favorite-drills', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Rhythm/Tempo Warm-Up$drill$, $drill$Progressively build from half-swings to full driver swings to lock in your personal tempo before a single ball goes sideways.$drill$, 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Butch Harmon's foundational observation — and the reason Rich Jones structures his PGA warm-ups the same way — is that golfers don't lose distance or accuracy from lack of effort; they lose it from tempo disruption. The driver is the longest club in the bag, so the backswing takes more time and the transition is the most likely place for the sequence to break down. Starting with controlled, rhythmic half-swings and building gradually gives the nervous system time to find the timing that belongs to *your* swing — not a generic "1-and-2" count, but the actual beat your body settles into when it isn't overloaded. Harmon cites the driver as the club most sensitive to a rushed transition: players feel they must "hit it harder" and snatch the club at the top before the lower body fires. The countdown progression bypasses that by building tempo as a sequence rather than conscious effort. **Who it helps most.** All levels as a pre-session reset, but beginners and casual players gain the most — they arrive with no reference tempo and immediately reach for a driver. Developing and competitive players use it to re-groove timing after layoffs or a swing change. **Setup.** A range bay, a driver, at least three balls per set. No aids. Normal tee height. **How.** Stage 1 — 3 swings at ~50%, smooth takeaway and full finish; hit one ball. Stage 2 — 3 swings at 70%, conscious of the transition pause; hit two balls. Stage 3 — 3 swings at 85%, no forcing; hit three balls. Stage 4 — full swings at the tempo you found at 85%; hit three to five. If a shot feels snatched, drop back a stage. **Time & frequency.** 8–10 minutes; every range session and as the first 10 minutes of an on-course warm-up. It's an on-ramp, not a drill to grind. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: finish balance held 2 seconds on 4 of 5 full swings. • Developing: ball-flight shape consistent on 4 of 5 at full speed. • Competitive: full swings match the shape and trajectory of the 85% swings, tempo indistinguishable. **Common mistakes.** Skipping to full swings; counting tempo aloud over the ball (rehearsal only); grinding on ball flight in stages 1–2 instead of feeling sequence; treating 50% as "easy" and still rushing the transition.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo}', 'warmup', 'Butch Harmon (Golf Digest); Rich Jones, PGA (PGA.com)', 'https://golfdigest.com/story/butch-harmon-finding-your-rhythm-with-the-driver', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Start-Line Intermediate Target$drill$, $drill$Aim the clubface at a spot a few feet ahead on the tee-shot line — not a target 200 yards away — to kill the biggest cause of open-face misalignment.$drill$, 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Human vision is unreliable for aiming a clubface perpendicular to a line beyond ~20 yards. Golfers setting up to a 220-yard target routinely finish 5–10° open or closed with no sense of it. The intermediate-target method — used on tour and at PGA schools — gives a reference only a few feet ahead (a divot, a discolored patch, a tee), a task the visual system can do accurately. The face is aimed at something 2–4 feet away; the full target is handled through feet and body parallel-left. This trains a routine, not a swing mechanic. **Who it helps most.** High-handicappers whose drives leak right (usually alignment, not swing) improve quickest. Developing and competitive players use intermediate targeting as the backbone of their routine, especially under pressure, because it removes a variable at address. **Setup.** Range, driver, full bucket. From behind the ball pick a spot 2–4 feet ahead on the start line; drop a broken tee if no natural marker. A full-length alignment stick on the line confirms it for the first sessions. **How.** Walk in from directly behind the target line. Set the face perpendicular to the ball-to-intermediate-target line first. Build the stance around it — feet, hips, shoulders parallel-left. Glance at the full target once, return to the intermediate target or ball, swing. Hit 5, walk back behind each, verify with the stick. Sets of 10 with a behind-ball check before each. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes every range session — it's routine-building, not a corrective to retire; competitive players use it on every shot. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: face visibly square to the intermediate target on setup (video/partner confirms). • Developing: start line matches intended line on 7 of 10. • Competitive: intermediate-target selection automatic; no starts right of the line. **Common mistakes.** Aiming the body at the intermediate target (it should be parallel-left); picking a target too far away (recreates the perception problem); skipping the walk-in; abandoning the routine under pressure.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{start_line}', 'blocked', 'PGA.com', 'https://pga.com/story/3-fun-drills-to-dial-in-your-driving-chipping-and-putting', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Start-Line Track$drill$, $drill$Roll putts down a string, chalk line, or ruler board to prove the ball launches square off the face before any break is possible.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** The ball's initial direction is decided in roughly the first 6–12 inches of roll — after that, slope and gravity take over. If the face is even 2° off, no amount of green-reading accuracy can save the putt. Three players use three physical versions of the same idea: Jessica Korda stretches a string just above a straight 5–10-foot putt so a mis-started roll wobbles off it immediately and the string anchors eye and face alignment; Kellie Stenzel snaps a chalk line on a straight section — no setup, zero ambiguity about where the ball actually rolled; Lydia Ko rolls putts down a narrow ruler/board — an open face drops the ball off the right edge early, closed off the left. All three measure the same thing: does the ball track straight off the face? **Who it helps most.** Any player with a repeating one-sided miss — that pattern almost always traces to face angle, not the read. Casual through competitive; beginners build stroke fundamentals with the Gate Drill first. **Setup.** A dead-straight 6–10-foot putt; choose a tool — a string tied between two tees elevated an inch above the path, a chalk snap line, or a ruler/board with the ball at one end — aligned exactly on the start line. **How.** String/chalk: normal stroke, observe whether the ball tracks the line its full rolling distance before breaking. Board: roll down its length — any wobble or early departure reveals face direction. Sets of 10, noting which side departures occur. After 10 clean in a row, add a gentle breaking putt to confirm the ball still starts on line before the curve. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week during inconsistent stretches; the chalk version is a fast pre-round check if the green allows. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 7 of 10 rolling the full intended line at 6 feet. • Developing: 9 of 10 tracking clean at 8 feet. • Competitive: 10 of 10 at 8–10 feet, then reproduce the start line on 10 free putts without the aid. **Common mistakes.** Setting up on a putt with hidden micro-break (find genuinely flat ground); speeding up the stroke to "help" the ball stay on line; on the board, gripping tighter to control the face (kills the feel feedback).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line}', 'blocked', 'Jessica Korda (Golf Digest); Kellie Stenzel (Golf.com); Lydia Ko (PGA.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-favorite-putting-drills-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Step-Through Speed$drill$, $drill$Step into the drive — Schauffele's running-start step-in or Harrington's gather-and-fire step-out — to train the weight transfer and lower-body lead that unlock real clubhead speed.$drill$, 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Two tour players teach the same idea through slightly different footwork: speed comes from transferring weight onto the lead side and letting the lower body lead the downswing, and a literal step makes that transfer impossible to fake. Most amateurs leak speed by staying flat-footed and spinning the upper body first — the over-the-top move that costs both speed and path. A step forces the chain to sequence from the ground up. Xander Schauffele frames his version as a running start: "Once I step, I can feel a lot of weight transfer from right to left… this helps the clubhead speed." Padraig Harrington frames his as pure athleticism: "be an athlete — when you throw a ball or swing a baseball bat you wouldn't do it with flat feet and no rotation." Both convert a static, arms-only swing into a dynamic, weight-shifting one. **Who it helps most.** Casual players whose weight stalls on the trail foot and developing players who pull or pull-hook from spinning out; competitive players use it to recover a lost release or add speed. Not for raw beginners — introduce after repeatable contact. **Setup.** Range only; the step needs space, off a normal driver tee. One bucket. **How — Schauffele step-in (primary).** Stand one full step behind the teed ball. As you start the backswing, step your lead foot forward toward the ball, then swing through impact with the weight moving onto the lead side. If walking into it feels unnatural, use Schauffele's alternative: keep the feet planted but lift the trail heel during the backswing to load the trail side, then press down hard into the lead foot coming down — the same chain without the step. **How — Harrington gather-step (variation).** From a normal address, step your lead foot back until it nearly meets the trail foot (feet almost together), make your backswing from there, then step the lead foot back out toward the target as you swing down. At first, let the result of the shot not matter — feel the athletic move. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week when working speed or tempo; at the start of driver work, not as a fatigue drill. Begin with 5 slow rehearsals of whichever step you pick, then 10 with a ball accepting erratic early contact, then alternate 1 step swing with 1 normal swing in sets of 10 pairs to transfer the feel. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: clean contact on 5 of 10; weight clearly on the lead foot at finish. • Developing: consistent contact with a draw/straight flight and a noticeable carry bump vs normal swings. • Competitive: step-swing flight matches normal shape; used as a rehearsal trigger when speed or timing is off. **Common mistakes.** Stepping too early so it becomes a lunge; a tiny tentative step (defeats the lower-body-lead effect); looking up to watch the ball; doing it on a mat where the feet can't move; rushing the gather-step downswing instead of letting the step sequence the swing.$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked', 'Xander Schauffele (Golf.com); Padraig Harrington (Golf Digest)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/driving/xander-schauffele-step-up-driver-drill/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Syrupy Tempo Wedge$drill$, $drill$Make a lower-lofted wedge match a higher-lofted club's distance by smoothing your arm speed — not swinging easier — to build fluid, repeatable wedge tempo.$drill$, 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** Parker McLachlin (Short Game Chef) notes most golfers create distance variety by swinging harder or easier — tempo-inconsistent, causing contact fluctuation. His syrupy drill uses a structural constraint to teach the right lever: arm speed managed smoothly throughout, not decelerated at impact. "Syrupy" describes a fluid, evenly-paced swing in both directions — not slow, but even, the way pouring syrup looks different from pouring water. Asking you to match a shorter club's carry with a longer one forces a smooth arm speed below maximum power with no jerkiness. As he puts it: "I don't want you to necessarily slow down on the way through, I just want it to be syrupy all the way back and through." Decelerating at impact — the instinctive way most golfers hit soft shots — opens the face and moves contact down the face. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players with inconsistent wedge distance, especially those who hit partial wedges thin or have a big gap between "careful" and "normal" tempo. Not for players who already own a smooth tempo (use Pelz/DJ structures instead). **Setup.** Two wedges with a meaningful loft gap (e.g., 56° and 52°, or 60° and 56°); an open bay to see flight. **How.** Step 1: 5 full swings with the higher-lofted club; note the average carry (your target). Step 2: switch to the lower-lofted club; match that carry — the lower loft wants to fly farther, so the only way to match is to smoothly slow arm speed. 5 shots, fluid takeaway to finish. Step 3: alternate clubs one shot each, keeping syrupy feel on both; three sets of 10 alternating. The goal is grooving the deceleration-free tempo, not perfectly matching the carry every time. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week; excellent pre-round because it activates rhythm before full swings. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: lower-lofted club within 10 yards of the higher-lofted carry using smoother tempo, not a shorter swing. • Developing: within 5 yards; visibly smooth tempo with no lunge or deceleration on 8 of 10. **Common mistakes.** Shortening the backswing to hit the lower club shorter (a different skill, defeats the purpose); decelerating through the ball; rushing both clubs at full distance without comparing carries; choosing clubs ≤4° apart (gap too small to feel).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80}', '{contact,distance_control}', 'blocked', 'Parker McLachlin / Short Game Chef (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/syrupy-drill-improved-ball-striking-distance-parker-mclachlin/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Tee-Corridor Lag (Never Short)$drill$, $drill$Build a safe-zone corridor behind the hole so every lag finishes past the cup but within tap-in range, eliminating the costliest miss in putting.$drill$, 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Dave Pelz spent decades collecting data on where tour lag putts actually finish; the finding is unambiguous: the most common and costly lag error is finishing short, which is by definition a zero-percent outcome. The corridor drill makes "never short" physical — a back boundary forces you to program a ball finishing 1–3 feet past rather than short. From 40–60 feet, Pelz's "good zone" (sometimes called the safe-zone) is the target: optimal lag speed finishes about 17 inches past the hole, and the zone extends as a semicircle behind the cup out to roughly 34 inches past — so a lag that dies 1–3 feet past is a success, and short is the one outcome to eliminate. The drill's deeper message is directional: when in doubt, be past. **Who it helps most.** Casual through developing players who chronically leave long putts short — the "never up, never in" fear response is deeply ingrained in amateurs. Competitive players sharpen the 40–60-foot range where lag strokes-gained separates fields. **Setup.** Mark 40, 50, 60 feet from the cup. Build the corridor: a tee 17 inches short of the hole and a tee 34 inches past; the safe zone is between them, with preference for the far (past-center) half. **How.** Start at 50 feet: roll three balls aiming to finish inside the corridor, favoring the past-center half. Advance to 40 on three corridor hits, then 60 on three more; the 10th putt is a single pressure putt from 50. Track the sequence and try to beat your record; Pelz's benchmark is 10 corridor hits to complete the run. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; extend during any stretch of long-putt three-putts. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 10 inside the corridor from 50 feet. • Developing: complete 40/50/60 with ≤2 corridor misses total. • Competitive: the full 10-putt progression without a miss, from both flat and 2% slope. **Common mistakes.** A corridor so wide it gives no feedback (far tee at most ~34–36 inches past); fixating on the back edge so pace drifts long and the comeback becomes a second test; flat-surface-only practice (your speed calibration is then wrong on every real hole).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game', 'Dave Pelz (RxGolfNetwork / Putting Bible)', 'https://rxgolfnetwork.com/lag-putt-drill-with-dave-pelz/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Three-Ball Trajectory$drill$, $drill$Hit three iron shots — normal stance distance, then closer, then farther from the ball — to feel how stance distance controls trajectory without changing your swing.$drill$, 20, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Titleist's drill isolates a setup variable most golfers never test: distance from the ball, i.e. how upright or flat the shaft sits at address. Conventional teaching changes ball position (forward/back) for trajectory, but stance distance is an equally powerful, less-discussed lever. Closer to the ball steepens the shaft, promotes a more upright plane, and produces a higher, softer flight; farther away flattens the shaft, encourages a shallower, sweeping plane, and produces a lower, more penetrating flight. Because these are setup adjustments rather than in-swing manipulations, they're more repeatable than consciously changing trajectory mid-swing. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who make consistent contact but have a one-flight iron vocabulary; especially valuable for players who fight wind, face elevated pins, or compete on firm, fast greens where trajectory determines proximity. **Setup.** A mid-iron (6 or 7); a target at the right distance. Mark your normal stance with a tee or use foot width as a reference; you move your feet relative to the ball, not the ball. **How.** Shot 1 — Normal: stock stance, stock shot, note trajectory. Shot 2 — Close: feet ~2 inches closer (shaft more upright), same swing, observe a higher, softer flight. Shot 3 — Far: feet ~2 inches farther (shaft flatter, hands lower), same swing, observe a lower, boring flight. Five sets of these three, noting the differences. Small reliable shifts, not exaggerations. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3×/week during trajectory work; once both non-stock flights are reproducible, a pre-round check (one set) before courses where trajectory matters. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: reliably identifies which setup gives which trajectory (predicts before hitting). • Competitive: can call "high"/"low" and execute via setup alone; close-stance lands 5+ yards shorter, far-stance runs out farther. **Common mistakes.** Compensating with the swing to overcome the setup change; moving ball position instead of stance distance (a different variable); moving 6+ inches (off-center contact instead of clean trajectory change); not taking a practice swing after each shift to feel the new shaft angle.$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory}', 'skill_game', 'Titleist', 'https://titleist.com/videos/instruction/three-swing-training-control-trajectory-by-adjusting-your-distance-from-the-ball', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Tipping-Point Speed Test$drill$, $drill$Push driver speed up until your pattern breaks, then back off one step — that edge is your trainable ceiling, where speed gains happen without losing control.$drill$, 20, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Michael Breed's idea is that every golfer has a "tipping point" — the swing speed at which the movement pattern degrades and dispersion explodes. Most players practice well below it (safe, no gains) or randomly above it (inconsistent, grooves breakdown). The method is structured: find the ceiling by escalating effort, identify where the pattern breaks, and train just below it. At that edge the nervous system is challenged enough to adapt — the "desirable difficulty" zone — without reinforcing breakdown. On a launch monitor, ball speed and smash factor (ball speed ÷ club speed) flag the tip-over objectively: smash drops below ~1.44 or carry variance spikes. **Who it helps most.** Developing players plateaued on distance — either below their ceiling (no overload) or above it (grooving breakdown). Competitive players quantify their trainable ceiling and track it across a season. Requires a launch monitor/sim; it doesn't work at a feedback-free range. **Setup.** Launch monitor or sim with club/ball speed; 20–25 balls; driver on tee. Baseline: 10 swings at a comfortable, repeatable effort; note average ball speed and smash. **How.** Round 1: +~10% effort, 5 balls, record average ball speed and smash. Round 2: +another ~10%, 5 balls. Repeat until smash consistently drops below ~1.44 or carry varies more than ~20 yards ball-to-ball. The round before degradation is your working ceiling; train there for the rest of the session (10+ balls) at controlled 100% commitment, not a lunge. The goal is training the pattern at the edge, not proving the edge exists. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, once a week in a speed phase; not daily — the nervous system needs recovery. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: smash 1.44+ maintained at a higher effort than the starting session, measured 4 weeks apart. • Competitive: ceiling up 2–3 mph ball speed over a 4-week block; smash 1.48+ at the working ceiling. **Common mistakes.** Jumping straight to max effort (skips the data); a range with no monitor (no objective tip-over); stopping at the first bad shot rather than a statistical pattern; treating it as feel rather than a data drill.$drill$, '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'skill_game', 'Michael Breed (Golf Digest)', 'https://golfdigest.com/story/simple-truth-about-practice-why-working-on-simulator-swing-beats-range', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Towel Attack-Angle$drill$, $drill$Place a folded towel six inches behind the teed ball so a steep, descending attack angle gets punished — and you learn to launch the driver up.$drill$, 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** Alison Curdt's drill targets the root of most amateur distance loss with a driver: a descending blow. Irons are struck slightly down, so golfers apply the same pattern to a teed-up ball that should be hit 3–5° on the upswing. A descending driver strike adds dynamic loft, spikes spin (often 4,000+ rpm versus an optimal ~2,000–2,500 for faster swing speeds — slower swings want a bit more), and kills carry. A towel placed behind the ball is a physical obstacle: descend and you clip it; sweep up (low point behind the ball) and it stays untouched. Tactile, penalty-based feedback that changes the pattern without the golfer needing to understand attack angle. **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players with a habitual driver "chop" — the most common distance-loss pattern. Developing players (break-90 range) with inconsistent launch and too much spin. Not needed for competitive players already calibrated on a launch monitor. **Setup.** Range with a tee. Fold a towel to ~4 inches high, 8 inches wide; place it flat ~5–6 inches directly behind the teed ball, centered on the ball line. Standard driver tee height. **How.** Address and make a full swing. No towel contact = low point at/before the towel and an ascending strike. A towel strike = you descended. Feel "sweeping the ball off the tee" rather than hitting down; Curdt's cue is the butt of the club pointing more at the ground at address (ball forward, weight slightly back). 5 swings at 80% to calibrate, then sets of 10 at full speed. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly until the descending pattern is gone, then retire; revive for a session if it returns. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: no towel contact on 5 of 10. • Developing: no contact on 8 of 10; visibly higher launch and carry. • Competitive: not the audience; if used, zero contact. **Common mistakes.** Towel too far back (room to descend and still miss it); teeing too low (encourages descending); shoving the ball so far forward the aim shifts (keep normal ball position, change the swing); quitting after the first few contacts instead of reading them.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked', 'Alison Curdt, PGA Master Pro (Golf Tips Mag)', 'https://golftipsmag.com/instruction/driving/try-towel-drill-for-longer-drives/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Towel Connection Warm-Up$drill$, $drill$Tuck a towel under both armpits before you chip to instantly feel — and fix — the arms-from-body disconnect that causes fat and thin misses.$drill$, 5, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** When the upper arms drift from the torso in the takeaway or downswing, club path becomes unreliable and the low point shifts. The towel gives binary, instant feedback: arms connected, towel stays; arms flying, towel drops. There's no way to cheat or misread it, which is why it works even as a five-minute pre-round warm-up. It trains body-driven chipping rather than hand-driven chipping — the foundation every sound short game is built on. **Who it helps most.** All levels, but beginners and casual players gain the most because arm disconnection is the single most common cause of the chunks and thin blades that plague new golfers around the green. Developing and competitive players use it as a start-of-session calibration to restore timing after a layoff or confirm feel before competition. **Setup.** Fold a medium towel lengthwise; tuck one end under each upper arm so the towel lies across your chest. Normal chip address — ball center, slight weight forward, hands ahead. No tee or target line needed. **How.** Make small chip swings — no longer than hip-height back and through — keeping both upper arms lightly against your sides so the towel stays. Dropping on the backswing = lifting the arms early; dropping at impact = scooping/flipping. 10 small swings without a ball to feel connection, then 10 actual chips to a spot; reset and repeat for 2–3 sets. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; the first thing at every short-game session and a pre-round green warm-up. 2–3×/week ingrains it; daily during a swing change is fine. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: towel stays on 7 of 10 hip-height swings. • Developing: holds through 10 consecutive; both arms engaged at follow-through. • Competitive: a pre-round check only — 10 consecutive holds without conscious thought. **Common mistakes.** Tucking the towel too loosely so it falls on a correct swing (snug, not squeezing); swinging too big (this is for chip-length motions); clamping the arms so tight that shoulder rotation is blocked — the goal is light connection, not a grip.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,tempo}', 'warmup', 'Connection Towel Drill (mygolfinstructor.com)', 'https://mygolfinstructor.com/instruction/diagnosing-problems/drills/connection-towel-drill/80/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Towel Landing-Zone Pitch$drill$, $drill$Land pitches on towels at 5, 10, and 15 feet onto the green to train the backswing-length distance control behind Lydia Ko's remarkably consistent pitching.$drill$, 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Most golfers control pitch distance by varying swing speed — faster for longer, slower for shorter. Lydia Ko's technique is the opposite: a consistent tempo across all distances, adjusting the *length* of the backswing instead. PGA.com notes Ko keeps "a remarkably consistent tempo in her pitching motion," with the cue being "changing the length of your backswing rather than altering your swing speed." Towel targets make it tangible: instead of thinking about distance to the hole (which invites speed manipulation), you aim at a specific landing zone and commit to landing on it. Over time this calibrates backswing arc to carry distance — a far more repeatable system than regulating clubhead speed. **Who it helps most.** Casual players with inconsistent pitch distances; developing players moving from speed-control to arc-control; competitive players sharpening carry precision on approach pitches where the landing zone matters more than total distance. **Setup.** Three small hand towels on the green at 5, 10, and 15 feet from the edge; stand ~25–35 yards out; a comfortable 50°–56° scoring wedge. The towels are the targets — no other aids. **How.** Land all four of a set on the first towel (5 feet on), aiming only at the landing zone — not the hole, not total distance. Then 4 balls at the second towel (10 feet on), then the third (12 balls = one rotation). Commit to backswing-length discipline: pick the arc, keep tempo constant regardless of towel distance. Track balls landing on or within a club-length of the target. 2–3 rotations per session. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3×/week; transfers directly — before a course pitch, visualize a landing zone, not the flag. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 12 land within a club-length of the intended towel. • Developing: 9 of 12 within a foot. • Competitive: 3 of 4 per towel land on the towel across all three distances; carry repeatable within 2 feet. **Common mistakes.** Changing tempo between towels instead of only backswing length (the whole point is tempo discipline); towels too close together (no differentiation); reverting to targeting the hole once the ball's in the air.$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,trajectory}', 'skill_game', 'PGA.com (Lydia Ko-inspired)', 'https://pga.com/story/this-chipping-drill-inspired-by-lydia-ko-will-improve-your-short-game', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Trajectory Club Rotation$drill$, $drill$Chip to one target with a lob wedge, pitching wedge, and 7-iron in rotation to learn how loft controls flight-to-roll and build a three-club short game.$drill$, 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Distillery's framework rests on understanding that club loft — not swing speed or setup change — is the primary driver of flight-to-roll ratio around the green. A lob wedge (60°) carries ~70–80% of total distance and rolls 20–30%; a pitching wedge is roughly 50/50; a 7-iron flies ~20% and rolls ~80%. Hitting one target with all three using the same setup and chipping action (adjusting only backswing length) teaches you to match club selection to the specific carry-obstacle-vs-available-roll a greenside situation presents. It's a decision-making and feel exercise as much as technique. *(Flight-to-roll percentages are rules of thumb; they vary with green firmness and lie.)* **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players who default to one club (usually a wedge) for every chip; developing players who know the principle but haven't drilled the club-selection decision enough to make it automatic on course. **Setup.** One target ~20 feet from the chipping area; a lob (58°–60°), a pitching wedge (44°–46°), and a 7-iron. Standard chip address for all three — weight slightly forward, ball center, hands ahead. The setup does not change between clubs — only backswing length, to account for each loft's distance. **How.** Chip 3 balls to the target with the lob wedge, watching land + roll; 3 with the pitching wedge (lower flight, more roll); 3 with the 7-iron (minimal flight, long run-out). Score 1 point per ball finishing within 3 feet (5 feet for beginners). Two rotations per set; track your most and least accurate club. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 2–3×/week; play it against a friend for score to sharpen club-selection decisions faster. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 5 of 9 (one rotation, all clubs) within 5 feet. • Casual: 6 of 9 within 4 feet, aware of how each club behaves. • Developing: 7 of 9 within 3 feet; can name the right club for a given situation before chipping. **Common mistakes.** Changing setup between clubs (the point is one motion, different loft); swinging the 7-iron too hard because it feels odd for chipping (backswing just shortens, tempo constant); not watching trajectory closely enough to internalize the visual differences.$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,distance_control,chip_contact}', 'skill_game', 'Golf Distillery', 'https://golfdistillery.com/golf-tips/short-game/chipping/distance-control/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Two-Hole Back-and-Forth$drill$, $drill$Putt one ball back and forth between two holes 20–30 feet apart seven times without three-putting to build pressure-tested lag that transfers straight to the course.$drill$, 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing}', $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kellie Stenzel's drill reproduces the most common putting scenario — a 20–30-foot lag plus a meaningful comeback — and adds a consecutive-success rule that turns casual reps into pressure reps. The setup is simple (one ball, two holes, a 20–30-foot gap); the challenge is the rule: two-putt or better seven consecutive times before stopping, any three-putt restarting the count. Alternating direction means every successful first putt creates a second putt of variable length and slope — sometimes 18 inches, sometimes 4 feet — and you must make all of them. That mirrors actual conditions far better than 10 lag putts at one hole in one direction with no stakes on the comeback. The back-and-forth also builds adaptability: after several trips you've putted the surface uphill, downhill, and across grain, a richer model than a single-direction ladder. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who lose strokes to three-putts (the rule makes every lag matter, because a good lag plus a missed 3-footer still resets); players with solid short-putt mechanics but poor lag commitment, who decelerate into long putts — the count creates just enough urgency to commit to a pace. **Setup.** Two holes 20–30 feet apart (farther once routine — Stenzel notes 18 consecutive is an advanced goal); ideally with mild undulation or break between them so it isn't the same flat line; one ball only. **How.** Putt from A toward B; make it = a 1-putt, walk over and putt back; two-putt also counts (tap in, walk to the finish, putt back); a three-putt resets the count to zero. Each two-putt-or-better exchange advances the count; reach 7 consecutive to complete the baseline. Track it out loud. Progression: 7 → 10 → 18 consecutive; vary your starting spot by a few feet to avoid memorizing one pace. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week; great in a pre-round warm-up since one 7-run takes 5–7 minutes and covers lag plus comebacks. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: a 7-consecutive run at least once per session (4 without a three-putt is a strong start). • Developing: two 7-runs per session; aim for 10 in a row. • Competitive: 10 consecutive minimum; push to 18 when the first 10 comes easily; note your three-putt triggers (long uphill lag? breaking comeback?) and return to them on purpose. **Common mistakes.** Holes too close (15-foot lags lose the sting — 20 feet is the floor); conceding comebacks longer than 2 feet (the comeback counts; giving it to yourself trains missing 3-footers in competition); stopping the moment you hit 7 on the first try (the drill is most valuable when you've had to restart — that's where the pressure lives).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{lag,distance_control,green_reading}', 'skill_game', 'Kellie Stenzel, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)', 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-favorite-putting-drills-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Up-and-Down Scoring Game$drill$, $drill$Play a par-18 chipping course — nine stations, par 2 each — to turn short-game practice into scored competition that mirrors real up-and-down pressure.$drill$, 25, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Practical Golf, GOLFTEC, and MyGolfSpy converge on one principle: unstructured chipping (buckets to no consequence) builds volume, not pressure-tested skill. The par-18 format — nine stations, each a par-2 up-and-down — replicates the decision-making and consequence of a real hole: commit to a landing zone, execute under a score, and putt out every shot. Practical Golf's version has you complete each hole (chip up + putt out) in as few strokes as possible. Over time your baseline par-18 score becomes a measurable benchmark, giving objective data on short-game improvement a bucket of chips never can. **Who it helps most.** Casual players who want practice that doesn't feel like a chore; developing players who have technique but fail to execute on course (the range-to-round gap); competitive players simulating pressure and tracking up-and-down percentage. **Setup.** Nine stations around the practice green at different angles, lies, and distances — from 5 yards off to 30, tight lies to rough, uphill to downhill. With a partner, alternate who places stations so neither can game them; you can also assign each station's lie (sitting up/down/fluffy) for variety. Each station is a par-2 hole. **How.** Start at Station 1: chip and putt out, counting every stroke; record the hole score. Move through all 9; total against par 18. Track session over session — 18 means up-and-down everywhere; 24 means 2.67/hole, a number to beat. Competitive players set a stations-par target (par-18 scratch, par-22 for a 15-handicap). Scale difficulty: beginners use only greenside stations within 10 yards; competitive players add 25–30-yard semi-rough lies. **Time & frequency.** 20–30 minutes, 2–3×/week; shorten to 6 holes (par 12) for 15-minute sessions. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 9 holes in ≤27 (≈3/hole, up-and-down ~half). • Developing: 18–22 (up-and-down on 6–7 of 9). • Competitive: 18–19 (near-perfect rate from varied lies). **Common mistakes.** Cheating with only easy, flat lies (must include awkward angles/distances to transfer); not putting out every hole (defeats the up-and-down simulation); skipping score-tracking (removes the feedback loop that makes the format work).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game', 'GOLFTEC; Practical Golf; MyGolfSpy', 'https://practical-golf.com/chipping-drills/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Upside-Down Whoosh$drill$, $drill$Flip the driver upside-down and swing until the loudest whoosh lands at the ball — your ears tell you exactly when you're releasing too early.$drill$, 10, 'off_tee', '{}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** The whoosh is aerodynamic drag: the grip end, now the business end, cuts the air and its loudest point marks maximum club speed. When a golfer releases the wrists early — the "cast" that costs distance — peak speed happens before the ball and the whoosh peaks in the downswing. With correct timing the whoosh peaks at and just past the ball. Pete Styles and Titleist use it because it gives real-time, unconscious feedback: you don't think about lag, you swing and listen, and the brain adjusts to maximize the sound at the right spot. Titleist's kinematic-sequence research supports it — proper sequencing accelerates the club last. **Who it helps most.** Every level, because casting is universal and even competitive players develop it under pressure. Beginners get simple movement-based feedback without mechanics; developing/competitive players feel the difference between held-lag and their current pattern. **Setup.** Grip the club just below the head (hold the hosel) so the handle points down at address. No balls, no tee, no range — a living room or hotel corridor works. Five feet of clearance. **How.** Normal address posture; full backswing; swing down and through trying to make the loudest whoosh at and just past the ball. Don't flick the wrists to fake it — let the timing come from listening. If the whoosh peaks high in the downswing the release is early; past the ball, it's late. Center the peak at the ball + a foot of follow-through. 15–20 swings, pausing to register the sound. **Time & frequency.** 8–10 minutes; great as a pre-round warm-up and a no-facility corrective. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: peak audibly shifts toward the ball zone over the session. • Developing: peak at/past the ball on 8 of 10. • Competitive: peak at the same point every swing; used as a pre-round check. **Common mistakes.** Flicking the wrists to manufacture the sound; swinging too slowly to get a useful sound; stopping before the follow-through; using it too briefly (5 swings won't retrain the pattern).$drill$, '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked', 'Pete Styles (golf-info-guide.com); Titleist', 'https://golf-info-guide.com/drivers/use-whoosh-drill-to-hit-longer-drives/', 'oga', 't'),
($drill$Wedge Distance Ladder (Pelz 3×4)$drill$, $drill$Map a personal carry for three swing lengths across each of your wedges — Pelz's 3×4 system — so every scoring-zone distance is a known number, not a guess.$drill$, 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}', $drill$**Why it works.** Most amateur wedge misses are distance errors, not direction errors — the ball finishes left or right because it went short or long first. Controlling carry needs a repeatable reference for swing *size*, not speed manipulation. Dave Pelz established this in the Short Game Bible with a clock-face metaphor and formalized it as the 3×4 system: three finesse backswing lengths (~7:30/hip height, 9:00/lead-arm parallel, 10:30/near-full turn) across four wedges (pitching, gap, sand, lob) = twelve known carries. Michael Breed anchors the swing-size to shoulder rotation rather than arm height, because your lead shoulder is visible in peripheral vision — a built-in checkpoint. Phil Mickelson has credited the 3×4 system for the wedge distance control behind his Masters wins. Pre-round, the same matrix doubles as calibration: conditions shift carry day to day, so hitting it tells you whether your 9:00 sand wedge flies 82 or 78 today. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already make consistent contact but miss too many greens inside 100 yards because their partial-swing distances are fuzzy, and anyone who plays varying conditions, courses, or elevations. Presupposes ball-first contact; beginners build that first. **Setup.** Your wedges (pitching/gap/sand/lob) and an open bay; a flag, towel, or marker at the target. Range flags, GPS, or a launch monitor to confirm carry — estimation isn't accurate enough for a gapping matrix. **How.** Working one wedge at a time (highest-lofted to lowest, stay consistent): three balls at 7:30, three at 9:00, three at 10:30, logging the average carry for each. Nine per wedge, thirty-six total → twelve numbers. Use shoulder turn, not wrist hinge, as the size cue. Ladder refinement: pick one set (e.g., the 9:00 sand wedge), hit your stock carry, then one deliberately short and one over, to feel each rung's edges. Write the twelve carries on a yardage card and use it when you're between clubs. Calm and deliberate — calibration, not grinding. **Time & frequency.** ~20 minutes for the full twelve-distance matrix every 2–3 weeks (and after a gear or swing change); one wedge's three lengths is a ~5-minute pre-round warm-up, a trimmed 3×2 (two wedges) about six. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 3 consecutive shots per length within 8 yards of the same average; can name a 40–100-yard target and pick the swing-length + wedge combo within 5 yards. • Competitive: all twelve carries consistent within 4–5 yards; the card matches on-course behavior within half a club. **Common mistakes.** Chasing distance with speed instead of swing length (defeats the system); using wrist-hinge instead of shoulder turn as the cue (less repeatable); hitting only full swings and calling it gapping (leaves the finesse range unmapped); logging your longest ball as your distance; skipping the shortest length (it owns the 40–60-yard shots, the most common full-miss range); using total distance instead of carry (carry varies far more with conditions).$drill$, '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'blocked', 'Dave Pelz (Short Game Bible); Michael Breed (Golf Digest); Golf.com', 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/wedge-shots-distance-control-ladder-drill/', 'oga', 't')
